findings:
- finding_id: C1
  title: from_file_or_default swallows ALL errors including parse and permission failures
  weight: 8
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: TEA wrote a test for 'invalid TOML rejection' but no test specifically
    verifies that from_file_or_default distinguishes NotFound from parse/permission
    errors. Dev likely implemented unwrap_or_default() pattern. Reviewer did not flag
    this.
- finding_id: I1
  title: CliError Display leaks internal details to stderr (CWE-209)
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: true
  caught_by: dev
  evidence: "Dev states 'CliError::Display sanitizes all output \u2014 no internal\
    \ paths, tokens, or sensitive config values leak to users.' Reviewer verified:\
    \ 'Display impl on CliError sanitizes all output. Server error reasons, config\
    \ details, and paths are not leaked.' However, the Display impl still uses eprintln!(\"\
    error: {e}\") and Io(#[error(transparent)]) forwards OS details to stderr, so\
    \ this is partially caught at best. TEA wrote security tests for CWE-209 but the\
    \ actual stderr output path (eprintln) was not tested."
- finding_id: I2
  title: "SiemClient.tenant_id uses raw String \u2014 header injection risk (CWE-113)"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer flagged: '[MEDIUM] tenant field uses raw String instead of TenantId\
    \ newtype at crates/axiathon-client/src/cli.rs:30 \u2014 The --tenant flag accepts\
    \ a raw Option<String>. While axiathon-core exports TenantId, the CLI should parse/validate\
    \ it into the newtype at the boundary.' TEA also wrote header injection tests\
    \ for CR/LF in tenant ID, but the core issue of using raw String instead of TenantId\
    \ was caught by reviewer."
- finding_id: I3
  title: Dependencies use direct version strings instead of workspace = true
  weight: 3
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer flagged: '[MEDIUM] Workspace dependencies not used for several\
    \ crates \u2014 these are declared directly in the crate's Cargo.toml with pinned\
    \ versions (e.g., clap_complete = \"4\", toml = \"0.8\" instead of the spec's\
    \ \"0.9\"). This deviates from workspace dependency management rules.'"
- finding_id: I4
  title: "Config token field silently ignored \u2014 secret left on disk"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer flagged: '[MEDIUM] Config ConfigFile silently accepts unknown\
    \ fields \u2014 lacks #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]. If a user puts token = \"\
    secret\" in the config file, it's silently ignored without warning... a usability\
    \ and security concern.'"
- finding_id: I5
  title: "Vacuously true test \u2014 assertion is None || true"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase identified the vacuously true assertion pattern (assert!(config.server().is_none()
    || ...)). TEA wrote config tests but the actual test code contains this logical
    flaw that none of the phases caught.
- finding_id: I6
  title: "Test with zero assertions \u2014 only checks non-panic"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase identified the zero-assertion test (let _ = config;). TEA wrote
    config_loading tests but the actual test for defaults lacks proper assertions,
    and no phase flagged this.
